\begin{cases}
f(x) + 2f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) = 3x, \\
f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) + 2f(x) = \frac{3}{x}.
\end{cases}
\] We can solve this system to find \(f(x)\). Let's multiply the first equation by 2 and subtract the second equation from it: \[2(f(x) + 2f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right)) - (f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) + 2f(x)) = 2(3x) - \left(\frac{3}{x}\right),\] which simplifies to \[3f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) = 6x - \frac{3}{x},\] or \[f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) = 2x - \frac{1}{x}.\] Substituting this back into the original equation, we get \[f(x) + 2 \left( 2x - \frac{1}{x} \right) = 3x,\] which simplifies to \[f(x) + 4x - \frac{2}{x} = 3x,\] or \[f(x) = -x + \frac{2}{x}.\] We need to find the values of \(x\) such that \(f(x) = f(-x)\). Since \[f(x) = -x + \frac{2}{x}\] and \[f(-x) = -(-x) + \frac{2}{-x} = x - \frac{2}{x},\] we set \[ -x + \frac{2}{x} = x - \frac{2}{x}, \] which simplifies to \[2 \cdot \frac{2}{x} = 2x,\] or \[\frac{4}{x} = 2x.\] Multiplying both sides by \(x\) gives \[4 = 2x^2,\] or \[x^2 = 2,\] so \[x = \pm \sqrt{2}.\] The real solutions are \(\sqrt{2}, -\sqrt{2}\). </think> <answer>sqrt(2), -sqrt(2)</answer>

